1038 #edb/ref 17592186504645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179817 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating an Amount Given a Ratio" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179817 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179817 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0e44aa33-0aca-5cb2-8c89-6391240beed8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating an Amount Given a Ratio as a Fraction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b5406de3-483e-56c2-b9c6-73de6769552e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179889 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179889 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179889 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179889 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179889 #edb/ref 1045 "Further Reasoning With Equivalent Ratios" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179889 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179889 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179889 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179889 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186179889 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d591686e-75aa-506f-aade-856d6dea793a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180233 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180233 #edb/ref 1069 "q-59895" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180233 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180233 #edb/ref 1071 "There are $15$ students in a class, and $\\frac{2}{5}$ of them wear glasses. How many students **do not** wear glasses?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180233 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9aff327a-3ab5-5bdb-b2ab-437fac7b7394" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180233 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given that the ratio of students who wear glasses to the total number of students is $\\frac{2}{5}.$ \n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of students who wear glasses, and the denominator represents the total number of students.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $15$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\times 3}{5\\times 3}=\\frac{6}{15}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $6$ students who wear glasses. \n\nSo the number of students who don't wear glasses is\n\n$$\n15-6=9.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180636 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180636 #edb/ref 1061 637169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180636 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a4a7f682-c223-50f1-a3eb-6b1f79aad9b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180636 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180637 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180637 #edb/ref 1061 637170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180637 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "91f76525-9d6e-5ea9-a6a3-b6249f246583" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180637 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180638 #edb/ref 1061 637173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180638 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5f14a9d9-61a5-5432-ad78-2673e2e5e50e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180638 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180660 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, we can use ratios to find quantities that aren't explicitly given in the ratio.\n\nFor instance, suppose that the ratio of boys to girls in the fifth grade is $3: 4.$ If there are $15$ boys, how many students are there in total?\n\nNotice that the \"total number of students\" doesn't appear in the ratio. However, we can find the number of students by adding the total number of boys and the number of girls.\n\nWe start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}3}:{\\color{red}4}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}}{{\\color{red}4}}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of boys and the denominator represents the number of girls.\n\nWe know that there are $15$ boys, so we want to make a numerator of $15.$ We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 5}{{\\color{red}4}\\times 5} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}15}}{{\\color{red}20}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since there are $15$ boys in the fifth grade, there must also be $20$ girls. So the total number of students is \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}15} + {\\color{red}20} = 35\\,.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180660 #edb/ref 1065 2051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180660 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ca3cabae-8ebe-5879-b608-9d0ebdb233fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180660 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330167 #edb/ref 1080 "8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330167 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330167 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "19618e12-debd-5b4e-9bf1-429e65d1a0bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330758 #edb/ref 1080 "10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330758 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330758 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5a4d2098-760f-5acc-84b4-40651c95e415" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330771 #edb/ref 1080 "12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330771 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330771 #edb/ref 1